# Licensing

Zerg is licensed in **layers**, because the parts of it stand in different relationships to
the programs you write. The rule behind every line below is one question:

> **Does this code end up inside the binary you ship?**

What ends up in your binary is permissive, so that Zerg can be used to build anything,
including closed and commercial software. What does not — the compiler that produced it,
and the documents that define the language — is licensed to keep the language itself
coherent.

## The layers

| Part                                | Path                                | License                          |
| ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| **Runtime**                         | `src/runtime/`                      | MIT                              |
| **Standard library**                | `src/stdlib/`                       | MIT                              |
| **Compiler** (self-hosted and seed) | `src/compiler/`, `src/bootstrap/`   | GPL-3.0-or-later                 |
| **Specification & grammar**         | `docs/`, `GRAMMAR`                  | CC-BY-SA-4.0                     |
| **Everything else**                 | `examples/`, `scripts/`, `editors/` | MIT                              |

Full texts are in [`LICENSES/`](LICENSES). Each directory above carries its own `LICENSE`
file naming which one applies there.

## What the table does not cover: `test-data/`

`test-data/` is a **private submodule** holding the conformance corpus — the programs the
gates build, run and compare, and the reason a claim in the specification can be measured
rather than asserted. It is **not part of a release**: no release artifact contains it, no
program built with Zerg links it, and a source tree without it still builds the compiler.
The gates that read it say so and stand down when it is absent.

`.gitmodules` records where that repository lives so that whoever already has access can
fetch it in the right place. **A path in `.gitmodules` is a bookmark, not a grant** — it
conveys no license to the corpus and no right of access to it. The corpus is licensed, if
at all, by its own repository; none of the licenses above reaches it, and nothing in this
file should be read as offering it.

## What this means for a program you write in Zerg

**Your program is yours.** Compiling with Zerg does not place any license on your source
or on the binary you produce. The compiler being GPL does not reach your output — output
is not a derivative work of the compiler that produced it, exactly as it is not for GCC or
Clang.

**The runtime is linked into your binary, and it is MIT.** Every Zerg program contains the
runtime, so this is the layer that decides whether Zerg is usable at all for closed or
commercial work. MIT means: it is, with attribution and no further obligation. The same is
true of any standard-library module your program imports.

So: **write what you like, ship it how you like.**

## What this means if you change Zerg itself

**Changing the compiler and distributing it makes your changes GPL-3.0.** If you fix a bug
in `src/compiler/`, extend the emitter, or fork the toolchain and give the result to
someone else, that work carries the same license and must be offered as source. Using the
compiler does not trigger this — only redistributing a modified one does.

**Changing the runtime or the standard library carries no such obligation.** They are MIT:
fork them, vendor them, embed them, keep your changes to yourself.

**The specification is CC-BY-SA-4.0.** Quote it, translate it, teach from it, build a
different implementation against it — with attribution, and with derivative documents under
the same license.

## The name

"Zerg" as the name of this language, and the specification in `docs/` and `GRAMMAR` as its
definition, are the project's to steward. The licenses above grant rights to the code and to
the documents; they do not grant the right to call a different language "Zerg", or to
present a modified specification as this one. An independent implementation of the language
is welcome and needs no permission — it simply needs its own name, or a clear statement of
what it is.

This is the ordinary arrangement: the code is open, and the identity is not a free-for-all,
so that "written in Zerg" keeps meaning one thing.

## Contributing

A contribution is offered under the license of the directory it lands in — MIT for the
runtime, the standard library and the examples; GPL-3.0-or-later for the compiler;
CC-BY-SA-4.0 for the specification. There is no separate contributor agreement.

